## Finding and bounded BUILD path

The accepted five-minute current-source capture still fails AC-4's maximum-gap condition: 6,050 matched frames over 302.506666 seconds, 20.000523 fps mean cadence, 89.163715 ms maximum EOF gap, and 753 of 6,049 gaps above 60 ms. New same-run evidence narrows cadence divergence to after regular V4L2 dequeue/SOE cadence and before raw-pipe delivery. It does not support a separate Python reader backlog, CPU runqueue delay as the primary cause, or sustained pipe backpressure. The exact FFmpeg internal pacing/synchronization wait and output-writer thread remain unresolved. PTS is V4L2 SOE, not EOF; retain `timestampEof` as host-monotonic time sampled after complete raw-frame read.

Before any source change, BUILD should run the approved read-only, one-variable 600-frame FFmpeg output-pacing A/B: current output-flush default versus a diagnostic fixed argv adding only output-side `-flush_packets 1`. Hold the one-thread mode, queue sizes, filter, camera mode, frame count, and reader constant; save no frame payload and make no source edit. This is diagnostic only and cannot pass AC-4 or authorize a production argv change. The existing five-minute acceptance remains required after any later approved source change.

## Open evidence limits

- DQBUF trace recorded 602 successful dequeues for 600 outputs and lacked sequence fields, so exact dequeue-to-output frame identity remains unproven.
- Scheduler snapshots followed each read and add observer cost to the next interval; the exact FFmpeg internal wait and writer-thread attribution remain unresolved.
- The proposed output-flush A/B has not been run. It may narrow output-buffer pacing only; AC-4 remains failed on the accepted reproduction.
- AC-6 reconnect/sibling continuity and AC-8 four-camera soak/CPU headroom remain unverified hardware deferrals. The existing minor FFmpeg child resource-limit finding remains open.

